agent=indexer.agent
Claude finished the response\n\n\n\nArchitected LSP optimizations and agent security improvements\n\nArchitected LSP optimizations and agent security improvements\n\nYes — several, and one of them is actively corrupting what my script extracts. Let me check one interaction first.\n\n\n\n\n\nRan a command\n\nRan a command\n\nConfirmed gap — I read request_timeout_secs but never applied it. Fixing:\n\n\n\n\n\nRan 2 commands, created 2 files, read a file\n\nRan 2 commands, created 2 files, read a file\n\nThe one that matters most: inlayHints.maxLength: null. The default is 25 characters, so rust-analyzer truncates hints — Result<Vec<OvsdbRow>, TransactError> arrives as Result<Vec<Ovsd…. Since I splice hints directly into the chunk body, that ellipsis gets embedded verbatim. You'd be vectorizing truncation artifacts across every let binding in the repo. This is silent; nothing errors.\n\n\n\nThe rest, in rough order of impact for op-dbus:\n\n\n\ncargo.features: "all" — default indexes only default features. Any feature-gated module is invisible to rust-analyzer, so it produces zero symbols and my walker silently emits nothing for those files. With allTargets: true you also get #[cfg(test)] and bench targets resolving properly, which feeds the cfg(test) propagation.\n\n\n\nprocMacro.enable + attributes.enable — non-negotiable for your stack. #[dbus_interface], serde derives, #[tokio::main] are attribute macros; without expansion those items either don't appear in the symbol tree or appear with unresolved types, and hover returns {unknown}. buildScripts.enable matters for the same reason if anything is codegen'd from schema.\n\n\n\ncheckOnSave: false — pure win for a bulk index pass. My client never sends didSave, so cargo check would only burn CPU competing with the analysis you're actually waiting on. You still get rust-analyzer's own type/macro diagnostics into the diag payload field; you just lose clippy-tier lints, which aren't what that field is for anyway.\n\n\n\nhover.memoryLayout.enable: true — the interesting one. It puts size/align/offset/niche data into hover markdown, which lands in the doc field of every struct and enum chunk. For a codebase doing OVSDB wire encoding and D-Bus marshalling, having layout in the retrievable text is genuinely useful — "which structs have padding waste" becomes a semantic query.\n\n\n\nrequest_timeout_secs: 180 — I'd read this field but never applied it; requests were pinned at a 45s hard-coded default. Fixed in the script, so the config value now actually governs. On a cold workspace with proc macros, inlayHint over a large file will blow past 45s and you'd have silently lost hints for exactly the biggest files.\n\n\n\ncachePriming.enable: true is load-bearing for my wait_ready() — it gates on the rustAnalyzer/cachePriming progress token. Disable priming and the gate never satisfies; you'd sit until --ready-timeout expires and then index against a half-warm server.\n\n\n\nTurned off deliberately: chainingHints, parameterHints, expressionAdjustmentHints, lifetimeElisionHints. Good in an editor, pure noise in an embedding — parameter names at every call site especially, since they duplicate the callee signature you already have in the header.\n\n\n\nTwo additions beyond Rust. The bash entry uses file_patterns for extensionless files — run, finish, check — which is exactly the runit service layout, so your supervise scripts become indexable symbols rather than invisible files. And the agent config is a least-privilege annotator: code/read/grep/glob only, no shell, no write, with denies on .env, keys, and wg*.conf. Run kiro-cli agent create indexer, paste that body, then use --kiro-agent indexer — and once it's in place you can drop --trust-all-tools from my _chat() invocation, which is broader than this pipeline needs.\n\n\n\n\n\nLsp\n\nCode · JSON \n\n\n\nIndexer agent\n\nCode · JSON \n\n\n\nLspvec\n\nPY \n\nNot a config file at all — that's the indexer script itself. Standalone Python, lives wherever you keep tooling, not in .kiro/.\n\n\n\nbash\n\ninstall -m755 lspvec.py ~/.local/bin/lspvec\n\npip install --user voyageai qdrant-client 'pycozo[embedded]'\n\nrustup component add rust-analyzer   # if not already\n\nSo the three files split like this:\n\n\n\nfile  goes  read by\n\nlsp.json  <repo>/.kiro/settings/  kiro-cli and lspvec\n\nagent JSON  kiro-cli agent create indexer kiro-cli\n\nlspvec.py ~/.local/bin/lspvec you\n\nlsp.json is the shared one — that's the whole point of reading it instead of hardcoding server config. Kiro launches rust-analyzer from it for /code queries, lspvec launches the same binary the same way for bulk indexing.\n\n\n\nThen from the repo root:\n\n\n\nbash\n\ncd ~/src/operation-dbus\n\nexport VOYAGE_API_KEY=... QDRANT_URL=http://127.0.0.1:6333\n\nlspvec index --repo . --lang rust\n\nStart there — no --annotate, no --patterns. Confirm symbol counts look sane against the crate before spending Voyage tokens or kiro calls on the enrichment passes. lspvec stats will show you the fan-in ranking, which is a fast sanity check: if main or your reconcile entry point isn't near the top, call-hierarchy resolution didn't work and something's off before you scale it up.\n\n\n\nscript go directly into qdrant and cozo?\n\n\n\n\n\nDifferentiated connection architectures between networked and embedded databases\n\nDifferentiated connection architectures between networked and embedded databases\n\nYes, both — no intermediate files, no export step. But they connect very differently, and the difference bites.\n\n\n\nQdrant is a network service. qdrant-client talks HTTP/gRPC to --qdrant (default http://127.0.0.1:6333). It has to already be running; the script won't start it. It creates the collection on first run — named vector sized to --dim, cosine, on-disk, plus payload indexes.\n\n\n\nCozo is embedded. --cozo ./lspvec.cozo is a RocksDB directory the script opens in-process. No server, no port, no daemon. The path is created on first run.\n\n\n\nThat asymmetry has one practical consequence worth knowing before it confuses you: RocksDB takes an exclusive lock. You cannot run lspvec index and lspvec query at the same time against the same --cozo path — the second one fails to open. Same if you leave a Python REPL holding it. Qdrant has no such restriction since it's a real server.\n\n\n\nSo the write path per run is:\n\n\n\nsymbols + edges  ──→ cozo   (full rewrite every run, cheap, local file)\n\nembeddings       ──→ qdrant (hash-gated; unchanged chunks skipped)\n\nGraph goes in whole every time because edges shift with any edit. Only Voyage calls are incremental — that's what --incremental protects, and it reads the prior hashes from Cozo, so the two stores are coupled. Nuke lspvec.cozo and the next run re-embeds everything from scratch.\n\n\n\nGiven how you run things elsewhere, you may want Cozo as a standalone service instead of embedded — pycozo supports an HTTP client mode against cozo-server, which drops the lock problem and lets the reconciler read the graph while an index run is in flight. It's a small change to cozo_open() if you want it.\n\n\n\n/yes\n\n\n\n\n\nArchitected flexible client mode detection system\n\nArchitected flexible client mode detection system\n\nNow let me actually verify it against a live Cozo, both modes:\n\n\n\n\n\nUnearthed critical bug in pycozo Client default behavior\n\nUnearthed critical bug in pycozo Client default behavior\n\ndataframe=True is the default — that returns a pandas DataFrame, but my code indexes res["rows"]. That would silently break incremental mode. Fixing and testing live:\n\n\n\n\n\nRan 2 commands\n\nRan 2 commands\n\nLock hint works. But the HTTP path errored with 'host' — pycozo wants different args than I assumed. Checking:\n\n\n\n\n\nRan a command\n\nRan a command\n\nThe URL goes in options['host'], not path. Correcting:\n\n\n\n\n\nRan 2 commands, edited a file, read a file\n\nRan 2 commands, edited a file, read a file\n\nClient mode is in, and installing pycozo to test it surfaced a bug that would have cost you real money.\n\n\n\ndataframe=True is pycozo's default. With pandas installed, db.run() returns a DataFrame, not {"rows": [...]}. Every read in my code does res["rows"] — on a DataFrame that's a column lookup, so cozo_known_hashes would raise, get swallowed by its except, and return {}. Incremental mode would look like it worked while re-embedding the entire repo through Voyage on every single run. Silent, and only visible on your bill. All four engines now pass dataframe=False, verified round-tripping symbols and edges through both :memory: and rocksdb.\n\n\n\nSecond correction: pycozo's http engine takes the URL in options['host'], not the path positional. My first attempt threw a bare KeyError: 'host' instead of the connection error.\n\n\n\nUsage:\n\n\n\nbash\n\ncozo server --engine rocksdb --path ./lspvec.cozo --bind 127.0.0.1 --port 9070\n\nlspvec index --repo . --lang rust --cozo http://127.0.0.1:9070\n\nlspvec query "ovsdb transact construction" --cozo http://127.0.0.1:9070 --expand 2\n\n--cozo-auth / $COZO_AUTH for a token. Four forms are dispatched off the string: rocksdb dir, .sqlite/.db file, :memory:, or an http(s) URL.\n\n\n\nEvery open now does a ?[x] <- [[1]] probe so failures surface immediately with a specific hint rather than at the first real query. The lock case is the one you'll actually hit — I tested it, and RocksDB's native message is kIOError::kNone / No locks available, which tells you nothing. It now appends the exact cozo server command to serve that same directory instead.\n\n\n\nWorth noting the ordering trap with server mode: cozo server takes the lock on the directory, so anything still holding it embedded must be stopped first. If you're putting this under runit, that's a one-shot dependency — the server comes up before any indexer run, and the indexer becomes a client with no lock of its own.\n\n\n\n\n\nLspvec\n\nPY \n\n\n\n\n\nWant to be notified when Claude responds?\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nClaude is AI and can make mistakes. Please double-check responses.\n\nIndexer agent · JSON\n\n{\n\n  "name": "indexer",\n\n  "description": "Read-only code annotator for the lspvec pipeline. Emits JSON only.",\n\n  "prompt": "You annotate code for a retrieval index. You always reply with exactly one JSON object and nothing else: no prose, no markdown fences, no preamble, no trailing commentary. You never modify files, never run shell commands, and never ask follow-up questions. If you cannot answer, reply with the empty-but-valid form of the requested schema.",\n\n  "tools": [\n\n    "code",\n\n    "read",\n\n    "grep",\n\n    "glob"\n\n  ],\n\n  "allowedTools": [\n\n    "code",\n\n    "read",\n\n    "grep",\n\n    "glob"\n\n  ],\n\n  "toolsSettings": {\n\n    "read": {\n\n      "allowedPaths": [\n\n        "./**"\n\n      ],\n\n      "deniedPaths": [\n\n        "**/.env",\n\n        "**/.env.*",\n\n        "**/*.key",\n\n        "**/*.pem",\n\n        "**/id_*",\n\n        "**/secrets/**",\n\n        "**/.ssh/**",\n\n        "**/wg*.conf",\n\n        "**/*.wgconf"\n\n      ]\n\n    },\n\n    "grep": {\n\n      "allowedPaths": [\n\n        "./**"\n\n      ],\n\n      "deniedPaths": [\n\n        "**/.env",\n\n        "**/secrets/**",\n\n        "**/.ssh/**"\n\n      ],\n\n      "allowReadOnly": true\n\n    },\n\n    "glob": {\n\n      "allowedPaths": [\n\n        "./**"\n\n      ],\n\n      "allowReadOnly": true\n\n    }\n\n  },\n\n  "resources": [],\n\n  "useLegacyMcpJson": false\n\n}
